00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.119 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by Molly  McHugh who's a foreign policy and  strategy consultant and an information  warfare expert Molly for people who may  be here information warfare and think oh  we're just talking about hacking what  are we really talking about when we use  that term it's a it's a very good  question and I think it's one that's  been discussed a lot in the last few  months in the wrong way there is this  sort of focus on cyber attacks and  hacking and leaked information but  information warfare is really about  narrative and about ideas and about  putting information into a space in  order to achieve specific goals you know  for example during the early days of the  war in Ukraine the Russian invasion of  Ukraine you had these narratives about  Ukrainian Nazis and you know the  Russians needing to intervene to provide  security and that's a really good  example of information warfare where  it's it's using information to create a  pretext for other intervention and as  someone who has worked as an advisor to  mikhail saakashvili then the president  of georgia as you observe the different  allegations that were made with regard  to russian attempts to influence the  american election in november  did particulars of it strike you as  consistent with your experience and or  not consistent what was sort of general  reaction when these allegations started  flying I think for those of us that work  in the area none of this was  particularly new or surprising the  Russians have used this type of  information architecture and these type  of tactics against their opponents and  against countries that they believe they  need to weaken or influence in some way  for many years and and it's it's gotten  more advanced and their tactics are more  developed and easier to replicate  because of the internet but particularly  because of social media and the kind of  echo chambers that they've been able to  establish using Facebook and Twitter and  other  sort of media to kind of create the  impression of more support for ideas  that they are in favor of then there  really is but this is the types of  things that you saw during the u.s.  election which were the amplification of  particular narratives and stories that  were not necessarily things of  particular US interests or concern  before they became kind of social media  phenomenon it's very much the type of  very much the type of tactic and that  the Russians have used in other  countries in Georgia and Moldova and  Ukraine I'm in the Czech Republic now  they're using them in France and and  Germany as well but they do it for  specific political purposes to influence  elections in many cases or to influence  sort of social opinion or attitudes  toward Russia or toward the West but  they're getting very good at  streamlining these initiatives and  deploying them to new places in quick  fast easily replicable ways and we need  to be much smarter about how we identify  them and how we counter them talk a  little bit about that when you say when  you talk about the identification of  these techniques at what sort of  operational level do we need to be  thinking is is are we are we completely  operating at the wrong level if we're  trying to figure out which of two  American presidential candidates  Vladimir Putin would prefer be president  like is that sort of missing the plot  altogether there is a piece of this is  identifying what we refer to as just  information about facts or stories or  sort of put into space and influence how  people think that is a very specific  type of information warfare but it's the  least important of them in many ways  because it is easy to see in in some  respects what is a lie and what is true  the the more confusing part especially  for media to identify or to react to is  this idea that sometimes the information  itself does not matter it is the reason  that the information was put into a  space and a good example of that is this  leaked dossier that was passed around  from sort of out of London this former  mi6 operative had working in a private  capacity had compiled some information  about now president Trump's ties with  Russia and this is sort of an leaked to  the media  it doesn't really matter if anything in  there is true or not if it was real or  not if it was the FSB you trying to give  this guy bad information to feed into  his clients or not the point is that the  Russians want to create the appearance  that they have a deep relationship with  President Trump that they are  influencing what he does and how he acts  and that President Trump is not doing  very much to counter that counter that  idea that is really the fact that you  can see in the entire operation but it's  very hard to get past the true or not  analysis of information into the what  was the point of the action of  introducing the thing and it's it's a  different way to think it's very hard to  do but it's how we need to start  understanding how the Kremlin operates  but also how other governments and  individuals including to some extent  president Trump's administration are  violating this type of information  architecture right and that that's I  find that very interesting and I think  you wrote about this to some degree in a  great article that I recently read of  yours which is that when we talk about  when we analyze a specific obvious  untruth that is being parroted by the  Trump administration that the sort of  layer one is well what are what are the  facts of that particular story but that  there's this underlying I don't know if  I would call it a motive or sort of  reality or context where if you can get  people to just more generally be really  unsure of what they can believe as a  more general principle that that can  also play to the advantage of the  establishment or the party in power  right this idea that hey you know what  they may not believe what we're saying  but they'll question whether they can  believe anything can you talk about that  a little bit and how that can be useful  is to many to a great extent this is the  goal of the modern Kremlin backed  information warfare they're not trying  it's not the 80s anymore they're not  trying to put forward a vision or a  narrative of the world that is the  counter truth to us that is the one  thing that everyone should believe it is  very much trying to create this idea  that everyone is basically the same  we're all flawed nobody has the right to  speak or to judge or to speak truth for  anything that there is no truth it's all  just confusion and chaos and that is a  very effective way to to sort of put  forth some of these stories and  narratives and a very effective way to  counter the types of values and ideas  that the United States of America has  for many years tried to champion in the  world and you saw a great example of  that this past weekend in the interview  the president Trump was giving before  the Super Bowl there was sort of this  amazing exchange where he said if we  should if we can work with Putin on  fighting terror we absolutely should and  if we can work with the Russians to  fight Isis in the Middle East then we  absolutely should be doing that and  O'Reilly kind of countered to that but  Putin is a killer can we work with a  killer he's a killer and Trump didn't  dispute that he was a killer he just  said so are we Americans or killers too  and that's the kind of moral see that  the Kremlin echo-chamber has really  tried to create for in particular the  last 10 years but even before this idea  that no one is morally right that there  is just chaos and sort of confusion in  the world and coming through that what  you need is strength and certainty and  strong leaders and screw values and  morals and everything else what you need  is sort of strength and certainty of  leadership and that is where you have  figures like Putin and others sort of  stepping into the space offering  solutions and and outcomes that are not  democratic that are not inclusive that  do not champion tolerance but the  champion these sort of very dark  dystopian visions of the world that we  live  and what we should pursue this is sort  of inherently a question that that we  could really only speculate on at this  point but I I'm split when I observe  different events that take place under  the Trump administration some related to  Russia and some not and I go between  sort of two perspectives one is that  this is a very carefully calculated  action statement or whatever and like  the O'Reilly we're also not that great  or we're not you know we're also guilty  of some of this whatever the exact  wording was I go back and forth between  thinking it's incredibly calculated and  thinking that trumps clueless and  there's just sort of unintended  consequences that may end up benefiting  Russia or Vladimir Putin is it even  possible to clearly figure out whether  some of what's going on is calculated or  just ignorant and if you're talking and  his talking points I think it's hard to  know if they are his or if they were  presented to him by those around him as  things that he should be saying but I do  think there's tremendous calculation  behind specific aspects of the  information and narrative that he is  offering to the American people you saw  it in his inaugural address this idea of  American carnage that we live in this  terrible lawless country he's sort of  expanding this now with this new idea  that there's all these terrorist attacks  that none of us know about because our  dirty media doesn't report about it but  that the world is much more unsafe and  untrue than what we actually even know  and that is very much a tactic that you  see of sort of aspiring totalitarian  structures this idea that only I can  protect you only I see the threats to  you and I think that there's enough  coming from the President and from his  team on this topic that this to me is  the theme that runs through a lot of the  chaos that you see in the White House  yes there are all these executive orders  yes there are these sort of missteps and  false starts and  you know moving back from something but  the thing that runs through it is this  it's this idea of you know crime of  terrorism of dark forces working against  us that we can only protect ourselves  from by working with really bad guys and  walling ourselves off from the world and  only by accepting the tactics of sort of  civilian slaughter and bloodbath will we  be able to actually fight terrorism in  the way that we should be doing should  have been doing from the start this sort  of category is the centerpiece of what  they are presenting to the American  people and that is extremely worrying  because it points to a centralization of  power the belief in a security state and  the sort of structure under which they  will form these new alliances with  countries like Russia and others who are  not democratic who are not liberal and I  mean that in a small L way not in a in a  blue or red way but countries that don't  believe in the idea that individual  rights and freedoms matter in the world  that everything should be sacrificed to  the good of the state seems to often go  back to that broader reality when we do  an analysis of many different aspects of  what's going on with the Trump  administration we've been speaking with  Molly McHugh foreign policy and strategy  consultant information warfare expert  Molly really appreciate you coming on  today happy to do it thanks for having  me

